{"batchcomplete":true,"query":{"pages":[{"pageid":15730683,"ns":0,"title":"Hafnon","revisions":[{"revid":1369923969,"parentid":1313939031,"minor":true,"timestamp":"2026-08-18T01:28:40Z","size":3505,"sha1":"0d025259de0c2a3df2b7dd54dee8fe957aed73cf","slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","content":"{{Short description|Hafnium neosilicate mineral}}\n{{Infobox mineral\n|name=Hafnon\n|image=Hafnon.jpg\n|caption=Very small brown single crystals of hafnon from [[Bernic Lake]], Lac-du-Bonnet District, [[Manitoba]], [[Canada]]\n|category=[[Nesosilicate]]s\n|formula= {{chem2|HfSiO4}}\n| IMAsymbol   = Haf<ref>{{Cite journal|last=Warr|first=L.N.|date=2021|title=IMA–CNMNC approved mineral symbols|journal=Mineralogical Magazine|volume=85|issue=3|pages=291–320|doi=10.1180/mgm.2021.43|bibcode=2021MinM...85..291W|s2cid=235729616|doi-access=free}}</ref>|strunz=09.AD.30|system=[[Tetragonal]]|dana=51.05.02.02|class=Ditetragonal Dipyramidal (4/mmm ) <br/>[[H-M symbol]]: (4/m 2/m 2/m)|symmetry=''I''4<sub>1</sub>/amd|unit cell=a = 6.5725(7) Å, c = 5.9632(4) Å=; Z = 4|habit=Euhedral to irregular crystals|cleavage={???} Indistinct|mohs=7.5|luster=Vitreous|opticalprop=Uniaxial (+)|refractive=n<sub>ω</sub> = 1.930 - 1.970 n<sub>ε</sub> = 1.980 - 2.030|birefringence=δ = 0.050|streak=grey white|density=6.97|diaphaneity=Transparent|impurities=Often zoned with zircon. Forms part of zircon-hafnon series|colour=Orange-red, brownish yellow, rarely colourless}}\n\n'''Hafnon''' is a [[hafnium]] [[nesosilicate]] [[mineral]] with the idealized chemical formula {{chem2|HfSiO4}}.<ref name=\"Mindat\" /> It is the mineral form of [[Hafnium(IV) silicate|hafnium silicate]] and one of the few known minerals with essential hafnium.\n\n==Chemistry==\nHafnon forms a [[solid solution|solid-solution]] series with its [[zirconium]] counterpart, [[zircon]] ({{chem2|ZrSiO4}}). Several other variants exist, with compositions such as {{chem2|(Hf,Zr,Th,U,Y)SiO4}}.<ref>http://www.minerals.net/mineral/silicate/neso/zircon/hafnon.htm Minerals.net</ref> In nature, part of [[zirconium]] is replaced by the chemically similar hafnium, so natural zircon is never pure {{chem2|ZrSiO4}}. However, a zircon with 100% hafnium substitution can be synthesized and is known as hafnon.\n\nHafnon occurs as transparent red to red-orange [[tetragonal]] crystals, with a [[Mohs scale|Moh's]] hardness of 7.5.<ref name=\"Mindat\">{{Cite web |title=Hafnon |url=https://www.mindat.org/min-1792.html |access-date=2025-08-13 |website=www.mindat.org |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Hafnon Mineral Data|url=https://webmineral.com/data/Hafnon.shtml|access-date=2021-01-31|website=webmineral.com}}</ref>\n\nHafnon occurs naturally in [[tantalum]]-bearing [[granite]] [[pegmatite]]s in [[Zambezia Province|Zambezia]] (Mozambique), and in weathered [[pegmatite]]s at Mount Holland, [[Western Australia]].<ref>http://www.handbookofmineralogy.org/pdfs/Hafnon.PDF {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211020021909/http://www.handbookofmineralogy.org/pdfs/Hafnon.PDF |date=2021-10-20 }} Handbook of Mineralogy</ref> It has also been reported at locations in [[Ontario]], [[Quebec]], and [[Manitoba]], Canada; [[North Carolina]], United States, and [[Zimbabwe]].<ref name=Mindat/>\n\n==References==\n{{Reflist}}\n*Emsley, John. ''Nature's Building Blocks.'' Oxford, 2001. {{ISBN|0-19-850341-5}}\n*{{cite journal \n | journal =[[American Mineralogist]]\n | volume = 67\n | pages = 804–808\n | year = 1982\n | title = Crystal structure of synthetic hafnon, HfSiO<sub>4</sub>, comparison with zircon and the actinide orthosilicates\n | url = http://www.minsocam.org/ammin/AM67/AM67_804.pdf\n | author = J. A. Speer, B. J. Cooper }}\n\n[[Category:Nesosilicates]]\n[[Category:Tetragonal minerals]]\n[[Category:Minerals in space group 141]]\n\n{{silicate-mineral-stub}}"}}}],"contentmodel":"wikitext","pagelanguage":"en","pagelanguagehtmlcode":"en","pagelanguagedir":"ltr","touched":"2026-10-30T04:46:55Z","lastrevid":1369923969,"length":3505}]}}